// Individually written short passages. Poems and stories below are original.
export const readingLiterary=[]
const add=(skill,difficulty,passage,prompt,correct,wrong,explanation)=>readingLiterary.push({skill,difficulty,passage,prompt,correct,wrong,explanation})
const central=(d,p,c,w,e)=>add('Central Ideas and Details',d,p,'Which choice best states the main idea of the text?',c,w,e)
central('medium','The following is an original poem.\n\nThe ferry left its rope upon the pier.\nAll winter I mistook it for a tether,\nsomething that kept the empty landing here.\nIn spring a child uncoiled it for a game;\nthe harbor did not drift away.','The speaker discovers that an ordinary object need not bear the stabilizing significance the speaker assigned to it.',['The speaker concludes that the ferry cannot return without its original rope.','The child teaches the speaker a practical method for securing the landing.','The harbor’s safety depends on preventing children from playing near the pier.'],'The imagined tether cannot literally hold a harbor, and the child’s game leaves the harbor intact. The final line gently revises the speaker’s symbolic reading rather than offering boating instructions.')
central('hard','In a hypothetical study, researchers asked experienced craftspeople to explain how they judged whether a surface was smooth enough. Their verbal descriptions emphasized appearance, but recordings showed that they repeatedly ran their fingers across the material while working. When touch was unavailable, their judgments changed. The researchers concluded that descriptions of expertise may omit perceptual routines that experts use without explicitly naming them.','Experts’ accounts of their methods may leave out sensory practices that influence their judgments.',['Experienced craftspeople rely on appearance because touch supplies no useful information.','Verbal descriptions completely reproduce the routines used during skilled work.','The researchers showed that people cannot acquire expertise without explaining it aloud.'],'The difference between stated emphasis and observed touch, reinforced by changed judgments without touch, supports an unarticulated component of expertise. The alternatives reverse or overextend that finding.')
central('medium','In this original story, Celia takes her daughter to the house where she grew up. She points out the staircase, the low window, the place where a cupboard stood. Her daughter asks where Celia used to hide. Celia opens her mouth, then laughs: none of the places she has named is the house she remembers at night.','A child’s question reveals a difference between Celia’s factual description of a house and her personal memory of it.',['Celia discovers that every architectural feature of her childhood home has disappeared.','Celia’s daughter remembers the house more accurately than Celia does.','Celia decides that childhood memories can never be shared with another person.'],'The listed physical features differ from the emotionally significant hiding places the question evokes. The text neither says all features vanished nor grants the daughter prior knowledge.')
central('hard','A fictional scholar studying diagrams notes that simplification is often described as loss. Yet a diagram that omits decorative details may make a relationship easier to see. The scholar proposes asking not how much an image preserves overall, but which distinctions it preserves for a particular purpose. On this account, omission can be an active analytical choice rather than merely an incomplete copy.','The value of a simplified diagram depends on which relevant relationships it makes clear, not on retaining every visible detail.',['A diagram is accurate only when it reproduces all details of its subject.','Decorative details must be removed from every kind of scientific image.','Simplification always destroys the relationships an image was meant to explain.'],'The passage evaluates selection in relation to purpose and allows useful omission. It does not prescribe one simplification method for every image or equate complete copying with usefulness.')
central('easy','The following is an original poem.\n\nMy sister gave the orchard trees\nour distant cousins’ names.\nI learned the names before the fruit,\nthen learned, each autumn, which were sweet.\nNow every letter from abroad\narrives with branches in it.','The speaker connects family members living far away with familiar trees and seasonal experiences.',['The speaker finds that naming trees prevents them from bearing fruit.','The sister sends fruit abroad instead of writing family letters.','The speaker forgets the cousins’ names after learning to identify the fruit.'],'The cousins’ names become associated with orchard trees, so letters evoke branches and remembered tastes. The poem does not describe failed fruit production, shipments, or forgetting.')
central('hard','In an imagined history project, researchers compare a factory’s production charts with workers’ accounts of the same months. The charts show steady output; the accounts describe repeated changes in shifts, tasks, and equipment. Rather than treating the sources as contradictory, the researchers argue that apparently stable results may depend on continuous adjustments invisible in aggregate records.','Stable output can conceal substantial changes in the work required to maintain it.',['Workers’ accounts show that the production charts must have fabricated every total.','Steady production proves that factory routines remained unchanged.','Aggregate records necessarily preserve more detail about daily work than personal accounts do.'],'The proposed interpretation reconciles steady outcomes with changing processes. Neither source must be false; they record different aspects of the same period.')
central('medium','In this original narrative, Tomas is asked to donate a tool from his late father’s workshop. He selects the newest plane, still wrapped in paper. At the collection desk he turns back, walks home, and returns with a worn one. The museum label, he thinks, should have something to explain beyond the maker’s name.','Tomas comes to value evidence of a tool’s use over its pristine condition when choosing an object to donate.',['Tomas learns that the museum accepts only newly manufactured tools.','Tomas returns because he discovers that the first tool is broken.','Tomas refuses to let any object from the workshop leave his possession.'],'The shift from wrapped new plane to worn tool suggests a preference for use history that can be interpreted. No rejection, breakage, or refusal is described.')
central('hard','A hypothetical study compares explanations of the same unfamiliar device. One explanation names every component; another describes how changing one component affects the others. Readers of the second explanation identify fewer component names afterward but predict the device’s behavior more accurately. The researchers distinguish recognizing a system’s parts from understanding relations among them.','An explanation can support understanding of a system’s behavior without producing the strongest recall of its component names.',['Remembering component names guarantees accurate predictions of system behavior.','Readers of the second explanation learned no information about the device.','The study found that both explanations produced identical strengths and weaknesses.'],'The two measured outcomes favor different explanations. The second supports relational prediction while the first favors names, so neither outcome can substitute for the other.')
central('medium','The following is an original poem.\n\nThe road crew painted over\nthe crooked arrow near our gate.\nWe praised the clearer marking,\nthen found ourselves arriving late:\nwe had always turned before\nthe old arrow finished pointing.','A corrected public sign disrupts a familiar habit that had accommodated the old sign’s imperfection.',['The speaker concludes that clearly painted signs are always impossible to follow.','The road crew intentionally directs travelers away from their destination.','The old arrow was useful because it accurately showed the length of the road.'],'The travelers had adapted their timing to the crooked arrow. The new marking is praised yet changes that learned routine; the poem does not make a universal claim against clarity.')
central('hard','A fictional researcher examining community rulebooks finds many instructions for settling disputes but few records of the disputes themselves. She argues that the instructions are evidence of what organizers considered worth anticipating. They do not establish how often each problem occurred or whether the recommended remedies were used. The rulebooks therefore illuminate institutional concerns more directly than actual frequencies of behavior.','Rules about possible disputes can reveal organizers’ concerns without directly measuring how frequently those disputes occurred.',['The absence of dispute records proves that no disagreements took place.','Every problem mentioned in a rulebook occurred equally often.','The researcher treats the recommended remedies as a complete record of actual decisions.'],'The passage distinguishes anticipated concerns from observed frequency and implementation. The alternatives turn prescriptions or missing records into unwarranted certainty.')
central('easy','In this original story, Hadi keeps a chair beside the door for a neighbor who no longer visits. When a new tenant pauses there one evening, Hadi almost asks him to stand. Instead, he brings another cup and asks whether the room is warm enough.','Hadi begins allowing a place associated with an absent neighbor to serve a new relationship.',['Hadi removes the chair because it reminds him of an unpleasant visitor.','The new tenant refuses the hospitality Hadi offers.','Hadi discovers that the absent neighbor has returned unexpectedly.'],'The initial hesitation shows attachment to the old association; the cup and conversation make room for a new person. The other choices describe events absent from the story.')
central('hard','In a hypothetical trial, observers classify distant shapes more accurately after practicing with blurred images. Researchers then test the observers on sharply defined images and find no comparable gain. They argue that learning may tune attention to the information available under particular viewing conditions rather than uniformly improve all forms of visual recognition.','Practice can produce a benefit specific to the viewing conditions under which it occurs.',['Blurred-image practice guarantees better recognition under every condition.','Sharp images contain less visual information than blurred images.','The observers were unable to classify any shapes before training.'],'The benefit appears in blurred viewing but not sharp viewing, supporting condition-specific learning. The text does not rank total information content or report complete pretraining inability.')
central('medium','A fictional novelist describes keeping a list of unused titles. She does not treat the list as a set of failed names for finished books. A title may suggest a voice, a setting, or a problem before any story exists, and she sometimes begins a new work by choosing one whose implications she does not yet understand.','The novelist uses possible titles as prompts for creating work rather than merely as labels for completed stories.',['The novelist believes every title must summarize an already finished plot.','The list contains only titles rejected by publishers.','The novelist avoids beginning stories until every implication of a title is clear.'],'Titles can precede and generate stories in the process described. The other choices impose sources or requirements that conflict with the passage.')
central('hard','In an imagined reconstruction of an old dwelling, researchers first arranged rooms according to a modern distinction between work and leisure. Tool marks and movable partitions suggested a different pattern: the same spaces changed function across a day. The revised reconstruction therefore represented activities over time rather than assigning each room a single permanent purpose.','Evidence of changing daily uses led researchers to replace a fixed functional classification of the rooms.',['The dwelling contained no tools or partitions before researchers arrived.','The revised reconstruction assigned every room exclusively to leisure.','The researchers concluded that rooms had identical uses at every hour.'],'The text moves from fixed work/leisure categories to time-dependent use. It does not eliminate work or claim unchanging functions.')
central('medium','The following is an original poem.\n\nI saved the ticket for the view\nwe never saw: the mountain closed\nbehind the afternoon’s thick rain.\nYears later, it recalls instead\nthe station bench, your borrowed coat,\nthe long delay we did not mind.','A souvenir comes to preserve an experience different from the one it originally represented.',['The speaker regrets keeping an object that cannot evoke any memory.','The speaker eventually sees the mountain from the same station.','The ticket proves that the trip occurred in clear afternoon weather.'],'The ticket was saved for a missed view but later evokes shared waiting and comfort. The mountain remains unseen in the text, and rain is explicit.')
central('hard','A hypothetical team evaluates a digital transcription by counting corrected spelling errors. Another team asks whether readers can identify who is speaking when several people interrupt one another. A version that performs well on the first measure performs poorly on the second. The teams conclude that the definition of transcription quality must reflect the interpretive tasks the document is meant to support.','Different evaluation tasks can reveal different kinds of transcription quality.',['Correct spelling alone establishes that a transcription preserves every useful feature.','Identifying speakers is unnecessary whenever a document has been digitized.','The teams found that all versions performed equally well on both measures.'],'The divergent spelling and speaker-identification results motivate a task-dependent assessment. Neither technical correctness nor digitization guarantees every interpretive function.')

const infer=(d,p,c,w,e)=>add('Inferences',d,p,'Which choice most logically follows from the text?',c,w,e)
infer('hard','In a hypothetical study, a signal is detected only when an instrument’s sensitivity is increased. The same adjustment also increases false detections in blank samples. Researchers plan a second test using a different instrument before reporting that the signal is present.','The increased sensitivity makes the initial detection insufficient by itself to distinguish a real signal from an artifact.',['The second instrument is guaranteed to detect the signal.','False detections establish that every positive reading from the first instrument is wrong.','Increasing sensitivity necessarily reduces all forms of measurement error.'],'The changed false-positive rate introduces uncertainty about the positive reading. It does not guarantee either confirmation or universal error.')
infer('medium','In this original narrative, June says she wants the shop’s old sign removed. When the painter begins covering it, she asks him to leave the smallest letter until last. She spends the afternoon working at the window from which that letter remains visible.','June’s wish to replace the sign coexists with an attachment she has not fully expressed.',['June has decided that the new sign must use smaller letters.','June believes the painter cannot complete the work.','June’s sole purpose is to preserve an example of the sign’s typeface.'],'Her effort to delay and watch the remaining letter complicates her stated desire for removal. A precise design requirement or single motive is not established.')
infer('hard','An imagined experiment finds that a reminder increases completion of a task. The reminder both names the task and supplies a direct link to it. No condition names the task without supplying the link.','The experiment does not isolate remembering the task from easier access as explanations of the increase.',['The link had no effect because the message also named the task.','Naming the task alone would necessarily produce the same increase.','The experiment shows that every participant had forgotten the task.'],'The intervention combines salience and access. Without a separating condition, the improvement cannot be assigned uniquely to either component.')
infer('medium','A fictional researcher compares two sets of notebooks. The older notebooks preserve final totals but erase intermediate arithmetic. The newer notebooks preserve both. The researcher finds more visible calculation errors in the newer set.','The difference in preserved working may affect how many errors can be observed.',['The older writers necessarily made fewer errors in their working.','Every visible error changed a final total.','The newer notebooks contain no accurate calculations.'],'Intermediate work creates additional opportunities to observe errors. A higher visible count need not reflect a higher underlying error rate, and the outcomes of individual errors are not given.')
infer('hard','In a hypothetical study, observers who hear a label before seeing an ambiguous shape classify it differently from observers who hear the label afterward. Both groups eventually receive identical words and images.','The order of information can influence interpretation even when the eventual information is the same.',['Only the label-before group received verbal information.','The shapes were objectively different across the two groups.','Receiving identical information guarantees identical judgments.'],'The stated manipulation is sequence, and the outcome differs. The alternatives contradict matched materials or the observed difference.')
infer('medium','The following is an original poem.\n\nHe mended every broken cup\nand set each one where guests could see.\nThe chipped blue bowl he would not mend;\nit stayed beside him while he worked,\nits missing piece a place his thumb\ncould rest without his looking down.','The bowl’s imperfection may have a practical or personal value that repairing other objects does not eliminate.',['He is unable to repair any object made from blue material.','He displays the bowl because guests consider it his best repair.','He has abandoned his practice of repairing broken cups.'],'The thumb’s familiar resting place suggests value in the chip itself. The poem does not establish inability, guest preference, or abandonment of repair.')
infer('hard','A fictional historian finds a tool depicted in a painting made far from the workshop where similar tools survive. Painters sometimes copied images from pattern books, and the historian has not identified the painting’s model.','The depiction alone cannot establish that the tool was used locally where the painting was made.',['The tool was never used anywhere outside the surviving workshop.','The painter certainly copied a pattern book.','A painting cannot preserve any useful information about tools.'],'Copying provides an alternative route by which the image could appear. The evidence does not settle whether copying occurred or rule out local use.')
infer('medium','In an imagined lending program, requests increased after reminders were introduced. The number of distinct borrowers stayed the same, while repeat requests by existing borrowers rose.','The increase in requests does not demonstrate that the program reached more distinct borrowers.',['Every existing borrower made exactly one additional request.','The program’s collection necessarily became smaller.','No borrower found the reminders useful.'],'The added requests came from repeat use rather than a larger user count. The exact distribution of additional requests and users’ evaluations are not given.')
infer('hard','A hypothetical study finds that participants perform a familiar task poorly when asked to verbalize every step. Their performance recovers when they stop explaining aloud. The researchers do not test unfamiliar tasks.','The result does not establish that verbal explanation is harmful for learning tasks participants do not yet know.',['Verbal explanation can never help any participant.','The familiar task became impossible to perform without speech.','The study directly compared novice learning with expert performance.'],'The measured effect concerns concurrent explanation during a familiar task, not all learning situations. The alternatives generalize beyond or contradict the design.')
infer('medium','In this original story, Sima arrives early to a reunion and chooses a chair facing the entrance. When her old friend appears, she moves her coat from the next chair without interrupting her conversation. She has not brought a second coat.','Sima may have been keeping the adjacent chair available for her friend.',['Sima knows that no other guest will attend the reunion.','Sima intends to leave before her friend can sit down.','Sima has mistaken her friend’s coat for her own.'],'The seat’s deliberate release when the friend arrives supports the modest inference of reserving it. The other choices add unsupported certainty or contradict the coat detail.')
infer('hard','In a hypothetical trial, a material retains its strength after repeated dry heating but weakens after heating in humid air. The manufacturer’s proposed explanation concerns heat alone and predicts identical effects regardless of humidity.','The observed difference gives reason to investigate humidity or its interaction with heat.',['Humidity is proven to be the only cause of all weakening.','Dry heating and humid heating had identical effects.','The material becomes stronger whenever moisture is added.'],'A condition-dependent difference challenges a heat-only explanation with identical predictions. It motivates further investigation without uniquely establishing a mechanism.')
infer('medium','A fictional catalog lists an object under the name of the person who donated it. A later reader treats that name as the maker’s. The catalog’s introduction explains that donor names identify the source of acquisition rather than authorship.','The later reader’s attribution relies on misinterpreting what the catalog’s naming convention records.',['The donor and maker must have been different people.','The introduction identifies the maker of every object.','The catalog deliberately conceals all acquisition histories.'],'The category is donation source, not maker, so the inference is unwarranted. The donor could also have made it, but the naming convention does not establish that.')
infer('hard','An imagined researcher compares a very accurate model with a simpler model. The accurate model uses a measurement that cannot be obtained until after the decision the model is meant to guide. The simpler model uses measurements available beforehand.','Predictive accuracy alone may not determine which model is useful for the intended decision.',['The simpler model necessarily has higher measured accuracy.','Information obtained after a decision can always guide that same decision beforehand.','The accurate model has no possible use in any setting.'],'Timing makes the accurate model’s information unavailable for the specified use. That limits this application without proving universal uselessness or reversing accuracy.')
infer('medium','The following is an original poem.\n\nThe house was sold. I packed the clocks,\nthe plates, the winter shoes.\nAt dusk I carried out the key,\nthen waited at the empty gate\nas if the house might need\none last thing brought to me.','The speaker’s departure involves an attachment that practical completion of packing has not resolved.',['The speaker has forgotten to pack every clock in the house.','The buyer has requested that the speaker wait outside.','The house literally promises to return a missing object.'],'The completed packing contrasts with personifying the house and lingering at its gate. The inference is emotional reluctance, not a literal exchange or stated request.')
infer('hard','In a hypothetical comparison, readers prefer a summary that sounds certain to one that describes uncertainty. When tested on whether they can identify situations where the finding may fail, the uncertainty group performs better.','A preference measure alone could miss an advantage in understanding the limits of a finding.',['The certain summary was more accurate in every respect.','Readers who prefer certainty cannot understand any qualification.','The two summaries were tested only for visual attractiveness.'],'The second outcome favors uncertainty despite the preference ranking. It shows a task-specific understanding benefit, not a universal inability or total accuracy comparison.')
infer('medium','A fictional researcher finds matching phrases in two interview transcripts. The interviewers used the same prepared question sheet, and some transcript passages repeat its wording.','Some similarity between the transcripts may reflect the shared prompts rather than independently chosen wording.',['Both interviewees necessarily gave identical answers to every question.','Prepared questions make every interview response unreliable.','The interviewers did not use any questions from the sheet.'],'The common prompt is a specific alternative source of repeated wording. It does not establish identical complete interviews or invalidate all responses.')

const structure=(d,p,task,c,w,e)=>add('Text Structure and Purpose',d,p,task,c,w,e)
structure('hard','A hypothetical report describes a machine that classifies objects correctly under stable lighting. _Its accuracy falls sharply when the same objects are viewed under moving shadows._ The authors therefore distinguish recognizing the tested image pattern from recognizing the object across changing conditions.','Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence?','It introduces a limitation that motivates a more qualified interpretation of the initial success.',['It offers a second example of equally successful classification.','It defines the physical process that creates every shadow.','It establishes that the objects themselves changed between tests.'],'The same objects perform differently under changed lighting, limiting what the initial accuracy establishes. The sentence neither repeats the success nor changes the objects.')
structure('medium','In this original poem, the speaker considers an inherited compass.\n\nIts needle knows the north,\nnot where my father meant to go.\nI turn it in my palm\nand find the same direction\nwaiting in each position.','What is the main purpose of the text?','To contrast a device’s fixed guidance with the personal intentions it cannot recover.',['To explain how the speaker repaired a defective needle.','To argue that north changes whenever a compass is turned.','To establish the exact route the speaker’s father followed.'],'The compass reliably indicates north, but not the father’s intended destination. That distinction organizes the poem; no repair or historical route is supplied.')
structure('hard','A fictional critic says that an actor’s pauses initially seem to interrupt the speech. As the scene develops, the pauses occur whenever the character approaches a subject she has promised not to discuss. The critic concludes that the interruptions make an unspoken commitment perceptible to the audience.','Which choice best describes the text’s structure?','It revises an initial impression by identifying a pattern and explaining its dramatic significance.',['It maintains an initial criticism by listing random performance errors.','It traces a promise’s legal consequences across several historical periods.','It contrasts two actors before deciding that neither understands the speech.'],'The apparent interruptions become meaningful through their recurring relation to a forbidden subject. The text concerns one performance and an interpretive pattern, not random mistakes or a comparison of actors.')
structure('medium','A hypothetical study finds that two routes have similar average journey times. _One route, however, includes occasional delays long enough to make travelers miss their connections._ The researchers recommend measuring reliability in addition to average speed.','What is the main function of the underlined sentence?','It identifies a difference obscured by the shared average and relevant to the recommendation.',['It shows that the researchers calculated the averages incorrectly.','It explains why travelers never need connections.','It provides evidence that both routes have identical delay patterns.'],'Occasional severe delays distinguish the routes despite similar means and support measuring reliability. They do not establish an arithmetic error or identical distributions.')
structure('hard','An imagined essay opens by asking whether a repair restores an object to a previous state. It describes a patched sail whose added fabric changes its weight and a mended bowl whose seam alters its grip. The essay closes by proposing that repair can create a new usable state rather than simply reverse damage.','Which choice best describes the overall structure?','It poses a conceptual question, examines cases that complicate a simple answer, and proposes a revised account.',['It lists failed repairs to show that damaged objects can never be used again.','It explains the manufacturing histories of two objects in chronological order.','It introduces a definition and then applies it without any qualification.'],'The examples challenge restoration as exact reversal and motivate a new-state interpretation. Both repaired objects remain usable, and no detailed manufacturing history is given.')
structure('medium','In this original narrative, the narrator complains that her brother labels every cupboard. _Then she spends a morning in his empty apartment and finds the medicines without opening a single wrong door._ She returns the pencil she had borrowed to add a teasing label of her own.','Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence?','It supplies an experience that challenges the narrator’s earlier dismissive attitude.',['It confirms that every label makes the cupboards harder to use.','It explains why the brother removed all labels from the apartment.','It introduces an unrelated dispute over who owns the medicines.'],'The successful search reveals practical value in the habit she mocked, explaining her changed response. The sentence does not confirm inconvenience or introduce the other events.')
structure('hard','A hypothetical article distinguishes predicting an outcome from explaining its cause. A model may predict which seedlings survive by using location alone, even if location summarizes several unmeasured influences. The article argues that strong predictions do not automatically identify which influence would change survival if manipulated.','What is the main purpose of the text?','To clarify why predictive performance is insufficient by itself to establish a causal mechanism.',['To show that location can never help predict survival.','To demonstrate that every unmeasured influence has no effect.','To recommend replacing all predictions with unsupported causal guesses.'],'The example supports a distinction between useful prediction and identification of an intervention-relevant cause. It neither denies prediction’s value nor dismisses unmeasured factors.')
structure('medium','A fictional essay begins with a photograph of a crowded station and the caption “departure.” It then points to people delivering food, cleaning platforms, and greeting arrivals. It ends by asking how the caption directs attention away from activities that are visible but not named.','Which choice best describes the overall structure?','It presents a familiar label, identifies details that exceed the label, and examines how naming guides interpretation.',['It proves that the photograph was taken at a different station.','It reconstructs one traveler’s complete journey from a single caption.','It argues that the station contains no departing passengers.'],'The visible activities complicate the caption’s emphasis without disproving the existence of departure. The passage concerns framing, not location verification or a complete journey.')
structure('hard','A hypothetical researcher studies conversations in which speakers finish one another’s sentences. _The completions sometimes change the direction of an idea rather than merely supply a missing word._ She therefore treats such exchanges as possible joint composition, not simply evidence that listeners predicted a fixed ending.','Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence?','It offers a reason to consider an alternative interpretation of the conversational behavior.',['It establishes that every interruption is accidental.','It defines the exact grammatical category of every completed word.','It rejects the possibility that listeners understand one another.'],'The altered idea direction motivates joint composition as an alternative to prediction. The sentence does not generalize about all interruptions or deny understanding.')
structure('easy','In this original story, Pavel first hears his neighbor’s piano as an unwanted noise. During a week without music, he begins to notice the hour when it used to start. When the piano returns, he opens the window before remembering that he meant to close it.','Which choice best describes what the text emphasizes through its sequence of events?','Pavel’s changed response to a sound whose absence revealed its place in his routine.',['Pavel’s successful effort to stop his neighbor from practicing.','The neighbor’s decision to move the piano into Pavel’s home.','A steadily increasing dislike that remains unchanged throughout the story.'],'Absence makes the routine noticeable and the final action contrasts with the initial irritation. The music is not stopped permanently, and no piano is moved.')
structure('hard','An imagined historian notes that a city’s published budget lists money assigned to repairs. _Receipts show that some of those funds were never spent and others were redirected._ She uses the two records together to distinguish planned priorities from completed work.','What is the main function of the underlined sentence?','It explains why the budget alone cannot establish what work was actually carried out.',['It shows that budgets never describe intended priorities.','It establishes that all repairs were completed for free.','It introduces evidence identical in meaning to the budget totals.'],'The spending differences reveal a gap between allocation and execution, motivating comparison of sources. The budget can still show intended priorities.')
structure('medium','A fictional reviewer describes a field guide’s first edition as organized by visible color. She next explains that juvenile and adult forms of one species often have different colors. She welcomes a new index based on shared structural features, while noting that the original color index remains useful for quick browsing.','Which choice best describes the reviewer’s approach?','She identifies a limitation in one organizing method and supports a complementary method without dismissing the first entirely.',['She rejects every form of indexing because species change color.','She claims the new index makes all visual observation unnecessary.','She criticizes the first edition solely because it contains too few species.'],'The review balances a color-based limitation with the added structural index and retains a role for the original. It does not reject indexing or discuss species count.')
structure('hard','The following is an original poem.\n\nI asked the wall for silence.\nIt gave me back my question,\nsmaller, then smaller still.\nBy the time the echo ended,\nI had learned the room’s dimensions\nand forgotten what I meant to ask.','What is the main purpose of the final two lines?','To show that the speaker’s attention has shifted from the original request to an unexpected discovery.',['To prove that the wall answered the question’s intended meaning.','To explain how the speaker built the wall to suppress echoes.','To establish that the room has no measurable boundaries.'],'The echo reveals spatial dimensions and displaces the original intention. The lines neither claim a literal semantic answer nor describe construction.')
structure('medium','A hypothetical article argues that a game’s rules leave room for different strategies. _Players following the same rules can favor risk, caution, speed, or the preservation of options._ The article uses this flexibility to distinguish shared constraints from identical behavior.','Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence?','It illustrates variation that can occur within a common rule system.',['It identifies four rules that every player must break.','It shows that the players participated in different games.','It contradicts the claim that rules can constrain action.'],'Different strategies under the same rules illustrate constrained variety. The sentence does not say the rules vanish or that participants violate them.')
structure('hard','A fictional researcher describes a classification scheme that groups objects by their most common use. She then examines a single object used successively as a container, a weight, and a keepsake. She proposes that the scheme’s simplicity makes it useful for some comparisons but inadequate for tracing an object’s changing biography.','Which choice best describes the overall structure?','It introduces a classification, tests its limits with a case, and evaluates its usefulness in relation to a particular purpose.',['It establishes a universal classification that cannot have exceptions.','It denies that any object can change its function.','It compares three unrelated objects without discussing the scheme.'],'One object’s changing roles exposes a limit of fixed common-use categories. The researcher preserves some uses of the scheme rather than claiming either universal adequacy or total uselessness.')
structure('medium','In this original narrative, Ana returns a borrowed ladder polished and repaired. Her neighbor runs a hand over a rough patch Ana missed and smiles. Only then does Ana recall that the patch surrounds shallow initials his children carved into the wood one summer.','What is the main purpose of the final sentence?','To reveal why a feature Ana treated as an imperfection has significance for the neighbor.',['To show that Ana borrowed the ladder without permission.','To establish that the neighbor cannot safely use the ladder.','To prove that polishing always removes every meaningful mark.'],'The final memory supplies the rough patch’s personal association. It changes its meaning without establishing unauthorized borrowing, danger, or a universal effect of polishing.')

const cross=(d,p,task,c,w,e)=>add('Cross-Text Connections',d,p,task,c,w,e)
const response='Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to Text 1?'
const agreement='Based on the texts, the authors would most likely agree with which statement?'
cross('hard','**Text 1**\nAn imagined critic argues that the notes in a scientific notebook matter because they preserve what the researcher believed at a particular moment.\n\n**Text 2**\nAnother critic agrees that notebooks preserve earlier beliefs but emphasizes crossed-out passages. Corrections can reveal how those beliefs changed, provided the original entries remain legible.',agreement,'Preserving earlier entries can make notebooks useful evidence of the development of ideas.',['Every crossed-out statement was false when it was first written.','Only final published conclusions have value for studying research.','A notebook should be erased whenever its author changes an opinion.'],'Both accounts value access to prior thinking; Text 2 adds the value of visible revision. Neither equates revision with complete falsity or favors erasing the record.')
cross('medium','**Text 1**\nA fictional teacher recommends giving students examples before asking them to name a general rule.\n\n**Text 2**\nAnother teacher also begins with examples, but asks students to compare a near-miss example with a successful one so that a critical difference becomes apparent.',agreement,'Specific examples can help students develop an understanding of a general rule.',['Students must memorize a rule before encountering any examples.','Near-miss examples always prevent students from learning.','Successful examples contain no information about general principles.'],'Both start from examples. Text 2 specifies a comparison method but does not reject the shared premise.')
cross('hard','**Text 1**\nA fictional analyst says an exhibition’s value is shown by the number of visitors it attracts.\n\n**Text 2**\nAnother analyst asks whether visitors return with new questions or use the exhibit in later projects. These outcomes may differ between exhibitions with similar attendance.',response,'By proposing additional outcomes that attendance alone may fail to distinguish.',['By denying that visitors can be counted accurately.','By claiming that the least attended exhibition is always best.','By agreeing that equal attendance establishes equal value.'],'Text 2 adds measures of subsequent engagement without ruling out attendance as useful information or reversing its ranking universally.')
cross('medium','**Text 1**\nAn imagined translator favors retaining a song’s repeated final word because the repetition links its verses.\n\n**Text 2**\nAnother translator retains the repeated pattern but changes the final word when needed to preserve the song’s rhyme in the new language.','Which choice best describes a difference between the authors’ approaches?','They differ over whether preserving a linking pattern requires preserving the same repeated word.',['They disagree over whether the song contains verses.','They differ over whether translations should use any repeated patterns.','They disagree over whether the song can be translated at all.'],'Both preserve a verse-linking pattern; the disagreement concerns exact lexical repetition versus adapted rhyme. The alternatives invent broader disagreements.')
cross('hard','**Text 1**\nA hypothetical researcher argues that a model’s success on a difficult benchmark shows that it has learned the underlying skill.\n\n**Text 2**\nAnother researcher notes that the benchmark can be solved using a shortcut absent from other versions of the task. She recommends tests that remove that shortcut.',response,'By questioning whether the benchmark distinguishes the intended skill from an alternative way of obtaining the answer.',['By concluding that every successful benchmark result must be fabricated.','By claiming the difficult benchmark contains no useful information.','By accepting difficulty alone as proof that no shortcut is possible.'],'Text 2 identifies a competing explanation and proposes a discriminating test. It does not invalidate all benchmark information or allege fabrication.')
cross('medium','**Text 1**\nA fictional planner says a riverside path should be evaluated by how effectively it connects neighborhoods.\n\n**Text 2**\nAnother planner agrees that connection matters and also studies whether the path offers places to pause without obstructing through traffic.',agreement,'Connecting neighborhoods is a relevant consideration when evaluating the path.',['Places to pause necessarily prevent all movement along a path.','The path should serve only people making complete end-to-end journeys.','A single count of through travelers measures every relevant use.'],'The shared consideration is explicit in both texts. Text 2 adds a complementary use without denying connection or making universal claims about obstruction.')
cross('hard','**Text 1**\nAn imagined historian interprets a repeated prohibition as evidence that officials strongly opposed the prohibited behavior.\n\n**Text 2**\nAnother historian accepts that interpretation but cautions that repetition does not by itself reveal how frequently the behavior occurred or how often officials punished it.',agreement,'Repeated prohibitions can reveal official concerns without necessarily measuring the frequency of the behavior.',['Every repeated prohibition was enforced successfully.','A frequently prohibited behavior must have been the most common behavior.','Official concerns cannot be studied through written rules.'],'Text 2 accepts the concern interpretation and specifies its limits; Text 1 makes no frequency claim that conflicts with those limits.')
cross('medium','**Text 1**\nA fictional designer recommends minimal labels to reduce reading time.\n\n**Text 2**\nAnother designer argues that a slightly longer label can reduce the time needed to choose an action by explaining a distinction users would otherwise have to infer.',response,'By distinguishing time spent reading from the total time needed to make a decision.',['By claiming that longer text always requires less reading time.','By denying that users ever need to choose actions.','By agreeing that reading time alone measures every aspect of efficiency.'],'Text 2 proposes a tradeoff between local reading time and overall decision effort, not a denial of length’s reading cost.')
cross('hard','**Text 1**\nAn imagined critic calls a performance authentic because it follows the earliest surviving score closely.\n\n**Text 2**\nAnother critic uses authentic to describe a performance responsive to the work’s changing performance tradition, even when it departs from that score.','Which choice best identifies the basis of the disagreement?','The critics use different criteria for what counts as an authentic performance.',['One critic believes the score exists and the other believes it was destroyed.','Both critics define authenticity exclusively by audience size.','The critics disagree about whether music can ever be performed.'],'The dispute concerns the standard of authenticity: earliest score versus a living tradition. The texts do not disagree about existence or possibility.')
cross('medium','**Text 1**\nA fictional researcher favors interviewing people in their workplaces because nearby objects can prompt useful memories.\n\n**Text 2**\nAnother researcher notes the same benefit but adds that workplaces may make people reluctant to discuss conflict within earshot of colleagues.',response,'By acknowledging the benefit while identifying a context-dependent limitation of the method.',['By denying that objects can ever prompt memories.','By insisting that workplaces are always private.','By concluding that interviews cannot produce any historical evidence.'],'Text 2 concedes prompting and raises privacy as a competing concern. It neither dismisses interviews generally nor denies the original benefit.')
cross('hard','**Text 1**\nA hypothetical analyst treats a rise in an organization’s published activities as a direct sign of increased activity.\n\n**Text 2**\nAnother analyst notes that the organization hired a communications officer during the same period. Previously unreported activities may now appear in the publication.',response,'By offering a change in reporting as an alternative explanation for the published increase.',['By proving that the organization stopped all activities.','By showing that published records never mention real events.','By assuming that a communications officer must reduce activity.'],'Greater reporting coverage can raise the recorded count without an equivalent behavioral increase. The alternative does not establish a decline or make records worthless.')
cross('medium','**Text 1**\nA fictional curator arranges tools by material to help visitors compare manufacturing choices.\n\n**Text 2**\nAnother curator arranges tools by task to help visitors compare different ways of solving the same practical problem.','Which choice best describes the relationship between the approaches?','They organize the same kind of objects to support different comparisons.',['They require that every tool be made from the same material.','They disagree about whether tools can solve practical problems.','They classify different objects because one approach excludes all tools.'],'Material-based and task-based arrangements answer different questions about tools. Neither requires uniform material or rejects practical use.')
cross('hard','**Text 1**\nAn imagined scientist values a theory because it explains several observations with one mechanism.\n\n**Text 2**\nAnother scientist accepts that unifying explanation is valuable but argues that the theory must also make predictions that distinguish it from alternatives.',agreement,'Explaining several observations through one mechanism is valuable, though further evaluation may still be needed.',['A unifying theory cannot be tested against alternatives.','Any theory explaining existing observations is automatically correct.','Prediction is irrelevant whenever a mechanism is described.'],'Text 2 explicitly accepts unification’s value and adds a requirement. Text 1 does not state that unification rules out further tests.')
cross('medium','**Text 1**\nA fictional editor recommends replacing an unusual term with a common one so that readers can follow the sentence more easily.\n\n**Text 2**\nAnother editor notes that the unusual term marks a distinction the common term would erase. She favors a brief explanation instead of replacement.',response,'By proposing another way to improve accessibility while preserving a distinction she considers important.',['By denying that unfamiliar terms can make reading difficult.','By recommending the removal of every explanation from the text.','By agreeing that the two terms mean exactly the same thing.'],'The explanation addresses difficulty without losing the distinction. Text 2 does not deny unfamiliarity or endorse equivalence.')
cross('hard','**Text 1**\nA fictional historian uses a diary to reconstruct the order of events during one family’s journey.\n\n**Text 2**\nAnother historian uses the same diary to study which experiences the writer chose to emphasize or omit.','Which choice best describes the difference in their uses of the source?','One emphasizes the sequence of events, while the other emphasizes the writer’s selection and presentation.',['One treats the diary as handwritten, while the other treats it as printed.','One believes the journey happened, while the other argues that no travel occurred.','One studies the family, while the other refuses to consider the writer’s choices.'],'The text explicitly supplies different analytical questions, not conflicting beliefs about medium or event existence.')
cross('medium','**Text 1**\nA fictional coach uses a timed drill to identify which players can execute a skill quickly.\n\n**Text 2**\nAnother coach uses a changing scenario to identify which players can adapt that skill when conditions shift.','Which choice best describes the relationship between the drills?','They assess different aspects of performance rather than necessarily provide contradictory rankings.',['They measure exactly the same outcome under identical conditions.','They establish that fast execution makes adaptation impossible.','They show that neither speed nor adaptation can be observed.'],'Speed under fixed conditions and adaptation under change are distinct outcomes. Different rankings need not be logically inconsistent, and neither text claims incompatibility.')

const evidence=(d,p,task,c,w,e)=>add('Command of Evidence (Textual)',d,p,`Which finding, if true, would most directly ${task}?`,c,w,e)
evidence('hard','In a hypothetical materials study, researchers propose that shallow grooves improve a gripper’s hold by channeling moisture away from the contact surface, rather than merely by increasing surface area.','support the proposed moisture-channeling explanation','Grooves that drain moisture improve wet-surface grip more than equally large grooves whose ends are sealed, while the two designs perform similarly when dry.',['Larger grippers can hold heavier objects than smaller grippers.','The grooves are visible under a microscope.','The gripper can be manufactured from more than one material.'],'The draining-versus-sealed comparison separates the proposed moisture pathway from comparable surface area, and the dry comparison checks its condition-specific effect. The other findings do not isolate that mechanism.')
evidence('hard','A fictional researcher proposes that an unfamiliar warning symbol is ignored because users do not understand it, not because they fail to notice it. The symbol marks a step that otherwise requires no special training.','support the interpretation-based explanation','Users can accurately report having seen the symbol, but respond appropriately more often after receiving an explanation of its meaning without any change to its appearance.',['Making the entire page load faster reduces total task time.','The symbol contains two contrasting colors.','Experienced users visit the site more frequently than new users.'],'Seeing the unchanged symbol while benefiting from a meaning explanation favors an interpretation problem over a visibility problem. The other findings do not separate these possibilities.')
evidence('medium','In a hypothetical field experiment, researchers suggest that the position of an artificial marker helps animals relocate a hidden food source. They want evidence that the animals use the marker rather than a fixed memorized location.','support the marker-use hypothesis','When researchers move the marker while leaving other cues unchanged, the animals search near its new position rather than the old food location.',['The animals return to the field on several days.','The food source contains the same amount on each trial.','The artificial marker is painted a bright color.'],'Search shifting with the marker directly indicates use of its position. Repeated visits, constant food, and appearance alone do not establish which cue guides searching.')
evidence('hard','An imagined engineering team proposes that small irregularities in a panel reduce echoes by scattering sound, rather than by absorbing substantially more sound energy. The proposal concerns the direction of reflections, not just their loudness.','support the scattering explanation','The irregular panel redirects reflected sound into more directions than a smooth panel while measurements show similar total absorption.',['The irregular panel costs more to manufacture.','Both panels can be attached to the same wall.','Listeners prefer the irregular panel’s visual appearance.'],'More dispersed reflections with similar absorption distinguishes scattering from the proposed alternative. Cost, attachment, and appearance do not explain acoustic behavior.')
evidence('hard','A fictional historian argues that a public list of contributors records everyone who financed a project. The list was printed after construction, and it labels the named contributors as the project’s supporters.','weaken the historian’s interpretation','Private receipts identify several donors whose payments supported the project but whose names are absent from the list.',['The list contains names in alphabetical order.','Some named contributors gave different amounts.','The project was discussed in more than one newspaper.'],'Documented omitted donors directly challenge the list’s completeness. Ordering, unequal contributions, and press coverage do not establish missing financiers.')
evidence('hard','A hypothetical researcher attributes improved form completion to simpler wording. In the same redesign, required fields were reduced from twelve to six. The team compared completion rates before and after the combined changes.','weaken the attribution specifically to simpler wording','With field count held constant, the simpler wording produces no completion improvement, while reducing field count with wording unchanged does.',['The original form used a smaller typeface.','Some users had filled out other forms before the study.','The redesigned form was published on a weekday.'],'Separately varying wording and field count supports the competing explanation. The other observations do not directly isolate the proposed cause.')
evidence('medium','A fictional critic claims that an anonymous poem must have been written for a particular festival because it mentions a lantern procession associated with that festival.','weaken the identification of the occasion','Contemporary records describe the same kind of lantern procession at several unrelated celebrations in the region.',['The festival attracted a large audience.','The poem survives in two handwritten copies.','Some festival participants wrote poetry on other occasions.'],'The motif is not unique to the proposed festival, reducing its value for identifying the occasion. The other facts do not show that the same reference could point elsewhere.')
evidence('hard','In a hypothetical study, researchers claim that a new practice routine produces knowledge that transfers to unfamiliar problems, rather than improvement limited to repeated examples.','weaken the transfer claim','Participants improve on the practiced examples but perform no better than the comparison group on new problems requiring the same principle.',['Participants enjoy the new routine more than the comparison routine.','The practiced examples have varying lengths.','The routine includes several opportunities for feedback.'],'The key outcome is performance on unfamiliar applications of the principle. An advantage confined to repeated examples challenges transfer; enjoyment and procedure do not establish it.')
const quote=(d,p,c,w,e)=>add('Command of Evidence (Textual)',d,p,'Which quotation from the text most directly supports the stated interpretation?',c,w,e)
quote('medium','The following is an original narrative. A reader interprets the narrator as reluctant to acknowledge the importance of a small gift.\n\nI told Lena the wooden bird was too ordinary to keep. She offered to take it back. I placed it behind the lamp before she could reach the shelf. “It will only gather dust,” I said, and afterward moved the lamp so the bird was easier to see.','“afterward moved the lamp so the bird was easier to see”',['“the wooden bird was too ordinary to keep”','“She offered to take it back.”','“It will only gather dust”'],'Making the bird more visible contradicts the dismissive statements and supports concealed attachment. The quoted dismissals establish the outward claim, not the opposing feeling most directly.')
quote('hard','The following is an original narrative. A reader argues that Noor’s irritation includes admiration.\n\nNoor watched her apprentice rearrange the pieces without asking permission. “You have undone an hour’s work,” she said. Then she leaned closer. The new pattern used the broken piece Noor had set aside. She reached for a clean sheet and began copying it before calling the apprentice back to apologize for the disorder.','“She reached for a clean sheet and began copying it”',['“without asking permission”','“You have undone an hour’s work”','“to apologize for the disorder”'],'Copying the new pattern indicates valuing what the apprentice achieved despite the irritation. The other excerpts support the irritation or rule violation rather than the additional admiration.')
quote('medium','The following is an original poem. A reader interprets the speaker as recognizing a history hidden in an everyday sound.\n\nThe stair complained beneath my shoe.\nI nearly called for nails and glue,\nthen heard the note my mother knew\nwhen she came home through evening rain.\nI left the loosened board alone.','“then heard the note my mother knew”',['“The stair complained beneath my shoe.”','“I nearly called for nails and glue”','“I left the loosened board alone.”'],'The association with the mother connects the ordinary creak to remembered experience. Leaving the board alone is a consequence of that recognition; the quoted line most directly supplies the hidden history.')
quote('hard','The following is an original narrative. A reader claims that Eren’s explanation of his uncle’s habit changes.\n\nAs a child I thought he counted the cups because he trusted no one to wash them. Years later I watched him set a place for a visitor who had not arrived. He counted again, paused at the empty chair, and added a cup. Only then did I stop hearing suspicion in the numbers.','“Only then did I stop hearing suspicion in the numbers.”',['“As a child I thought he counted the cups”','“he trusted no one to wash them”','“He counted again”'],'The final sentence explicitly marks a revised interpretation of the habit. The earlier suspicion describes the view being replaced, and counting again alone does not state the change.')
evidence('hard','An imagined scholar argues that differences among copies of a song reflect adaptation to local performance, not only accidental copying errors. Those differences occur in both wording and rhythm across regional versions.','support the adaptation interpretation','Changed lines consistently supply local place names and fit the local performance’s rhythmic pattern.',['Copies sometimes differ in the color of ink used.','Some songs were written on larger sheets than others.','One copy contains a smudged letter that cannot be read.'],'Coherent local references and metrical fit suggest purposeful adaptation. Physical differences and an isolated smudge do not show meaningful changes for performance.')
evidence('medium','A fictional researcher claims that users misunderstand an instruction because it leaves the order of two actions unclear. They can identify both actions but sometimes perform them in different sequences.','support the order-ambiguity explanation','Users give two different action sequences when paraphrasing the instruction, and explicit numbered steps reduce that disagreement.',['The instruction is printed on recycled paper.','Users recognize the names of both actions.','The two actions can be completed with the same tool.'],'Divergent sequences and improvement after numbering directly address order. Knowing the action names or sharing tools does not establish sequence clarity.')
evidence('hard','In a hypothetical experiment, a team suggests that pauses in a sound help listeners divide it into meaningful units rather than simply make the recording easier to hear.','support the segmentation explanation','Moving the pauses changes which units listeners report, even when volume, clarity, and total pause duration remain the same.',['Turning the volume higher makes the recording louder.','The recording can be played on several devices.','Some listeners prefer shorter recordings.'],'Changing perceived unit boundaries with pause placement while controlling audibility supports segmentation. The other findings do not identify how the pauses influence interpretation.')
evidence('hard','A fictional historian proposes that a town’s imported materials were used chiefly for public buildings rather than distributed widely across households. She bases this proposal on inventories preserved by the municipal office.','weaken the proposed concentration in public buildings','Household repair accounts from many neighborhoods repeatedly list the imported materials in ordinary domestic work.',['A public building used imported stone in its main entrance.','The town maintained an inventory of public buildings.','Some imported materials cost more than local materials.'],'Widespread domestic records support broad household distribution, challenging a concentration chiefly in public construction. An individual public use supports rather than weakens the proposal.')

const vocab=(d,p,c,w,e)=>add('Words in Context',d,p,'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?',c,w,e)
vocab('hard','The fictional scientist preferred the more ______ explanation: it accounted for the same observations while requiring fewer independent assumptions, although she still regarded its predictions as needing further tests.','parsimonious',['ornate','equivocal','anachronistic'],'Parsimonious describes economy of assumptions. Fewer assumptions distinguish the explanations; uncertainty about later tests does not make equivocal the relevant comparative property.')
vocab('medium','In the original story, Lila tried to make the repair ______, matching the old paint and hiding the new fastening beneath the frame so visitors would not notice it.','inconspicuous',['conspicuous','provisional','inflexible'],'Inconspicuous means not readily noticeable, matching both concealment measures. The other choices concern visibility in the opposite direction, temporary status, or rigidity.')
vocab('hard','The fictional catalog’s omission was ______ rather than deliberate: the editor discovered that a damaged index card had caused the object to be skipped and immediately restored the entry.','inadvertent',['strategic','irrevocable','methodical'],'Inadvertent means accidental or unintentional. A damaged card explains the omission without intent, and immediate correction contradicts irrevocability.')
vocab('medium','The fictional committee adopted the temporary shelter as an ______ solution, choosing a workable response that could be implemented before winter rather than waiting for its preferred permanent design.','expedient',['ornamental','inaccessible','inevitable'],'Expedient means suitable for an immediate practical purpose. The committee selects a feasible short-term measure; ornament, lack of access, and unavoidable necessity are not its stated basis.')
vocab('hard','The imagined debate concerned the mechanism behind a measured effect. Questions about the report’s cover design were ______ to that dispute: they neither distinguished the proposed mechanisms nor changed the measurements.','peripheral',['decisive','inseparable','foundational'],'Peripheral means secondary or outside the central issue. The design questions do not bear on the mechanism debate, unlike the central roles implied by the other choices.')
vocab('medium','In the fictional workshop, the exchange was ______: the apprentice taught the master a new digital method, while the master taught the apprentice how to recognize a fault by touch.','reciprocal',['unilateral','redundant','incidental'],'Reciprocal means involving exchange in both directions. Each participant teaches and learns, unlike unilateral one-way transfer. The passage does not call either lesson unnecessary or merely secondary.')
vocab('hard','The original narrator is ______ about selling the boat: she welcomes freedom from its repairs but dislikes the thought of a harbor in which she has no reason to wait.','ambivalent',['indifferent','resolute','credulous'],'Ambivalent means having opposing feelings. Relief and anticipated loss coexist; indifference lacks either strong attachment, while resolute suggests a settled position.')
vocab('medium','The fictional researchers designed the prompt to ______ explanations rather than yes-or-no responses, asking participants to describe how they reached their decisions when they evaluated the same unfamiliar device.','elicit',['suppress','authenticate','duplicate'],'Elicit means draw out or obtain a response. Asking for reasoning is intended to produce explanations, not suppress, verify, or copy them.')
vocab('hard','The fictional critic argues that the novel’s setting and narrative voice are ______: the narrator’s vocabulary derives from the harbor’s work, and the harbor is perceived through that vocabulary.','inextricable',['interchangeable','incidental','chronological'],'Inextricable means closely connected in a way that resists separation. Mutual shaping supports it. Interchangeable would mean one could substitute for the other; incidental and chronological describe different relations.')
vocab('medium','The fictional report’s longest section was not necessarily its most ______ one. Readers repeatedly identified a brief paragraph about exceptions as the passage that most changed their understanding.','salient',['lengthy','obsolete','uniform'],'Salient means especially important or prominent. The contrast separates length from interpretive significance; lengthy merely repeats the already given length comparison.')
vocab('hard','The hypothetical study treated two stages as ______, measuring them separately because one involved detecting a signal and the other involved deciding what the signal meant.','discrete',['continuous','indistinguishable','simultaneous'],'Discrete means distinct or separate. The different operations motivate separate measures. Continuous and indistinguishable oppose the distinction, while timing is not the stated issue.')
vocab('medium','Repeated early successes made the fictional team ______; members stopped checking the calibration until a failed trial revealed that the instrument had drifted. They had previously recorded a reference reading before each session.','complacent',['vigilant','impartial','resourceful'],'Complacent means overly satisfied and insufficiently alert to problems. Neglecting checks after success illustrates it; vigilant is the opposite, and the other qualities are not established.')
vocab('hard','The fictional historian described the attribution as ______: it rested on one partially legible initial, while several makers in the district shared that initial and worked during the same period.','tenuous',['conclusive','redundant','unassailable'],'Tenuous means weakly supported. A limited, nondistinctive initial supplies little decisive evidence. Conclusive and unassailable overstate its strength; redundant means unnecessarily repetitive.')
vocab('medium','The fictional archive marked the painting’s date as ______ because two qualified specialists had proposed different decades and neither argument had yet resolved the disagreement.','contested',['anonymous','definitive','obsolete'],'Contested means disputed. The disagreement concerns the date, not the identity of the specialists, final certainty, or whether the date has become outdated.')
vocab('hard','A second hypothetical experiment ______ the researchers’ decision to preserve the unusual result: after an independent team repeated the procedure, the same pattern appeared again.','vindicated',['precluded','obscured','anticipated'],'Vindicated means justified in light of later evidence. Independent repetition supports preserving the original finding; it does not prevent, conceal, or merely predict that decision.')
vocab('medium','The fictional designer used contrasting type sizes to ______ the main instruction from optional background information on a crowded instruction card, giving the two categories a clearly visible boundary.','demarcate',['conflate','infer','imitate'],'Demarcate means mark a boundary or distinction. Contrast separates the categories, whereas conflate merges them; infer and imitate do not describe the design action.')
